1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,092
July 2nd, 1994.

2
00:00:01,092 --> 00:00:01,821
Medellín, Colombia.

3
00:00:01,821 --> 00:00:02,549
1 a.m.

4
00:00:02,549 --> 00:00:05,462
Andrés Escobar has just left a nightclub called

5
00:00:05,462 --> 00:00:06,190
El Indio.

6
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:08,010
He is twenty-seven years old.

7
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:10,923
He is, by every account of every person

8
00:00:10,923 --> 00:00:13,836
who ever knew him, one of the kindest

9
00:00:13,836 --> 00:00:15,292
men in Colombian football.

10
00:00:15,292 --> 00:00:18,205
A man who smiled easily, argued rarely, and

11
00:00:18,205 --> 00:00:21,118
carried himself with a grace that earned him

12
00:00:21,118 --> 00:00:24,031
a nickname he would never have chosen for

13
00:00:24,031 --> 00:00:24,395
himself.

14
00:00:24,395 --> 00:00:25,851
El Caballero del Fútbol.

15
00:00:25,851 --> 00:00:27,308
The Gentleman of Football.

16
00:00:27,308 --> 00:00:29,857
He walks out into the night air.

17
00:00:29,857 --> 00:00:32,041
And he is shot six times.

18
00:00:32,041 --> 00:00:34,954
Witnesses say that with every shot, the gunman

19
00:00:34,954 --> 00:00:36,410
screams the same word.

20
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:36,775
Gol.

21
00:00:36,775 --> 00:00:37,139
Gol.

22
00:00:37,139 --> 00:00:37,503
Gol.

23
00:00:37,503 --> 00:00:40,416
Ten days earlier, Escobar had scored an own

24
00:00:40,416 --> 00:00:42,236
goal at the World Cup.

25
00:00:42,236 --> 00:00:44,785
That is the story they told you.

26
00:00:44,785 --> 00:00:46,969
This is everything they left out.

27
00:00:46,969 --> 00:00:49,882
--- To understand what happened to Andrés Escobar,

28
00:00:49,882 --> 00:00:52,795
you first have to understand what Colombia meant

29
00:00:52,795 --> 00:00:54,616
in the summer of 1994.

30
00:00:54,616 --> 00:00:56,800
Not just as a football team.

31
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:57,893
As a symbol.

32
00:00:57,893 --> 00:01:00,805
This was a squad of genuine, uncommon brilliance.

33
00:01:00,805 --> 00:01:03,718
Carlos Valderrama, with his impossible hair and even

34
00:01:03,718 --> 00:01:06,631
more impossible vision, dictating play like a conductor.

35
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:09,180
Freddy Rincón, powerful and relentless in midfield.

36
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,093
Faustino Asprilla, a wiry forward who could burn

37
00:01:12,093 --> 00:01:14,641
a full-back alive with a single touch.

38
00:01:14,641 --> 00:01:17,554
And at the back — holding it all

39
00:01:17,554 --> 00:01:19,011
together — Andrés Escobar.

40
00:01:19,011 --> 00:01:19,739
Number 2.

41
00:01:19,739 --> 00:01:20,103
Centre-back.

42
00:01:20,103 --> 00:01:23,016
Captain's bearing, even when he wasn't wearing the

43
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:23,380
armband.

44
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:26,293
In September 1993, this team did something extraordinary.

45
00:01:26,293 --> 00:01:28,113
They went to Buenos Aires.

46
00:01:28,113 --> 00:01:29,206
To the Monumental.

47
00:01:29,206 --> 00:01:32,118
Into the bear pit of Argentine football, where

48
00:01:32,118 --> 00:01:35,031
the home crowd had never — not once

49
00:01:35,031 --> 00:01:37,944
— watched their team lose a World Cup

50
00:01:37,944 --> 00:01:38,308
qualifier.

51
00:01:38,308 --> 00:01:39,400
Colombia won 5–0.

52
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:39,765
5–0.

53
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:40,857
In Buenos Aires.

54
00:01:40,857 --> 00:01:43,770
The reverberations were felt all the way to

55
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:46,683
São Paulo, where a man named Pelé —

56
00:01:46,683 --> 00:01:49,595
the greatest player who had ever lived —

57
00:01:49,595 --> 00:01:52,508
watched the result and declared Colombia potential champions

58
00:01:52,508 --> 00:01:53,601
of the world.

59
00:01:53,601 --> 00:01:53,965
Champions.

60
00:01:53,965 --> 00:01:55,057
Of the world.

61
00:01:55,057 --> 00:01:57,970
A nation that had spent decades being defined

62
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:00,883
by its misery — by its cartels, its

63
00:02:00,883 --> 00:02:03,795
kidnappings, its war — was being told by

64
00:02:03,795 --> 00:02:06,708
the greatest footballer in history that its team

65
00:02:06,708 --> 00:02:08,165
could conquer the planet.

66
00:02:08,165 --> 00:02:11,078
You have to understand what that meant in

67
00:02:11,078 --> 00:02:11,442
Medellín.

68
00:02:11,442 --> 00:02:14,354
Medellín in 1993 was only just beginning to

69
00:02:14,354 --> 00:02:14,719
exhale.

70
00:02:14,719 --> 00:02:17,631
Pablo Escobar — no relation to Andrés —

71
00:02:17,631 --> 00:02:20,544
had been hunted across the city for years,

72
00:02:20,544 --> 00:02:23,457
a ghost of violence who had turned their

73
00:02:23,457 --> 00:02:26,370
home into the murder capital of the world.

74
00:02:26,370 --> 00:02:29,283
In 1991, the homicide rate in Medellín reached

75
00:02:29,283 --> 00:02:31,103
approximately 375 per 100,000 people.

76
00:02:31,103 --> 00:02:33,288
Football was not just entertainment here.

77
00:02:33,288 --> 00:02:36,201
It was the only counter-narrative a city could

78
00:02:36,201 --> 00:02:36,565
offer.

79
00:02:36,565 --> 00:02:39,478
And Andrés Escobar was the face of that

80
00:02:39,478 --> 00:02:39,842
counter-narrative.

81
00:02:39,842 --> 00:02:42,755
He had been a Nacional man since 1986.

82
00:02:42,755 --> 00:02:45,667
In 1989, he was part of the side

83
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:48,580
that won the Copa Libertadores — the first

84
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:51,493
Colombian club ever to lift South America's greatest

85
00:02:51,493 --> 00:02:51,857
prize.

86
00:02:51,857 --> 00:02:54,770
He had been to Switzerland to play for

87
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:55,862
BSC Young Boys.

88
00:02:55,862 --> 00:02:58,775
He was, by the summer of 1994, reportedly

89
00:02:58,775 --> 00:03:01,688
on the verge of signing for AC Milan.

90
00:03:01,688 --> 00:03:02,416
AC Milan.

91
00:03:02,416 --> 00:03:05,329
He was twenty-seven years old, and his best

92
00:03:05,329 --> 00:03:07,514
years were still ahead of him.

93
00:03:07,514 --> 00:03:09,334
Then came the World Cup.

94
00:03:09,334 --> 00:03:12,247
--- Colombia arrived in the United States in

95
00:03:12,247 --> 00:03:15,160
June 1994 carrying the weight of a nation's

96
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:15,524
expectation.

97
00:03:15,524 --> 00:03:18,437
But inside that team camp, something was already

98
00:03:18,437 --> 00:03:18,801
wrong.

99
00:03:18,801 --> 00:03:21,714
Head coach Francisco Maturana received death threats before

100
00:03:21,714 --> 00:03:24,627
the tournament had even started — threats delivered

101
00:03:24,627 --> 00:03:27,175
through the hotel switchboard, about team selection.

102
00:03:27,175 --> 00:03:30,088
Specific players the cartels wanted in the lineup.

103
00:03:30,088 --> 00:03:31,909
Players they wanted kept out.

104
00:03:31,909 --> 00:03:34,822
Maturana would later say he felt he had

105
00:03:34,822 --> 00:03:36,642
no choice but to listen.

106
00:03:36,642 --> 00:03:39,191
This was the world Colombian football inhabited.

107
00:03:39,191 --> 00:03:42,104
Not a world of tactics and training schedules,

108
00:03:42,104 --> 00:03:45,016
but a world where the wrong lineup could

109
00:03:45,016 --> 00:03:46,109
get someone killed.

110
00:03:46,109 --> 00:03:49,022
Where men with guns cared about who played

111
00:03:49,022 --> 00:03:49,750
left midfield.

112
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:52,663
Where the money wagered on a result by

113
00:03:52,663 --> 00:03:55,575
narco bosses was more dangerous than any opponent

114
00:03:55,575 --> 00:03:56,668
on the pitch.

115
00:03:56,668 --> 00:03:59,581
Colombia's football had been — for years —

116
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:01,765
funded, in part, by cartel money.

117
00:04:01,765 --> 00:04:04,678
Pablo Escobar himself had poured resources into Atlético

118
00:04:04,678 --> 00:04:07,591
Nacional, laundering drug profits through transfer fees, building

119
00:04:07,591 --> 00:04:08,683
stadiums, paying players.

120
00:04:08,683 --> 00:04:11,596
It had created one of the most talented

121
00:04:11,596 --> 00:04:12,688
squads on earth.

122
00:04:12,688 --> 00:04:15,601
It had also created an obligation that could

123
00:04:15,601 --> 00:04:16,694
not be refused.

124
00:04:16,694 --> 00:04:19,606
By the time Colombia flew to America, they

125
00:04:19,606 --> 00:04:21,791
were carrying passengers they hadn't invited.

126
00:04:21,791 --> 00:04:23,976
Their first match was against Romania.

127
00:04:23,976 --> 00:04:26,160
The Colombians were chaotic, open, unsettled.

128
00:04:26,160 --> 00:04:29,073
Gheorghe Hagi — one of Europe's finest players

129
00:04:29,073 --> 00:04:30,165
— was brilliant.

130
00:04:30,165 --> 00:04:31,258
Romania won 3–1.

131
00:04:31,258 --> 00:04:33,078
The dream was already wobbling.

132
00:04:33,078 --> 00:04:35,263
But it could still be saved.

133
00:04:35,263 --> 00:04:38,176
Win the next game — against the host

134
00:04:38,176 --> 00:04:41,089
nation, the United States — and Colombia could

135
00:04:41,089 --> 00:04:42,181
survive the group.

136
00:04:42,181 --> 00:04:44,001
The mathematics were still alive.

137
00:04:44,001 --> 00:04:44,365
Just.

138
00:04:44,365 --> 00:04:47,278
On June 22nd, 1994, Andrés Escobar walked out

139
00:04:47,278 --> 00:04:50,191
onto the Rose Bowl turf for the most

140
00:04:50,191 --> 00:04:52,012
important match of his life.

141
00:04:52,012 --> 00:04:54,924
He had no idea it would cost him

142
00:04:54,924 --> 00:04:55,289
everything.

143
00:04:55,289 --> 00:04:58,201
--- For the first half hour, it's level.

144
00:04:58,201 --> 00:04:58,566
Goalless.

145
00:04:58,566 --> 00:05:01,478
Colombia are playing — finally — with something

146
00:05:01,478 --> 00:05:02,935
like their real quality.

147
00:05:02,935 --> 00:05:04,391
Then, the 35th minute.

148
00:05:04,391 --> 00:05:07,304
American midfielder John Harkes picks the ball up

149
00:05:07,304 --> 00:05:08,760
wide on the left.

150
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:10,581
He drives towards the byline.

151
00:05:10,581 --> 00:05:13,494
He whips in a cross — low, hard,

152
00:05:13,494 --> 00:05:15,314
cutting across the penalty area.

153
00:05:15,314 --> 00:05:17,863
Andrés Escobar stretches to cut it out.

154
00:05:17,863 --> 00:05:18,955
He gets there.

155
00:05:18,955 --> 00:05:21,868
The ball catches the inside of his left

156
00:05:21,868 --> 00:05:24,781
foot — just the toe — and deflects

157
00:05:24,781 --> 00:05:27,694
past his own goalkeeper, Óscar Córdoba, into the

158
00:05:27,694 --> 00:05:28,058
net.

159
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:30,607
There is no malice in the moment.

160
00:05:30,607 --> 00:05:31,335
No recklessness.

161
00:05:31,335 --> 00:05:34,248
It is a routine defensive intervention that goes

162
00:05:34,248 --> 00:05:35,340
catastrophically, irreversibly wrong.

163
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:36,068
One touch.

164
00:05:36,068 --> 00:05:36,796
One instant.

165
00:05:36,796 --> 00:05:39,709
The United States go on to win 2–1.

166
00:05:39,709 --> 00:05:42,622
Earnie Stewart adds a second to make it

167
00:05:42,622 --> 00:05:45,535
2–0, before Colombia pull one back as a

168
00:05:45,535 --> 00:05:46,263
late consolation.

169
00:05:46,263 --> 00:05:48,084
But it is too late.

170
00:05:48,084 --> 00:05:50,997
And in that moment — in the parking

171
00:05:50,997 --> 00:05:53,909
lots outside stadiums, in the back rooms of

172
00:05:53,909 --> 00:05:56,822
Medellín nightclubs, in the offices where men in

173
00:05:56,822 --> 00:05:59,735
expensive shirts watched the game through a lens

174
00:05:59,735 --> 00:06:02,648
of money wagered and money lost — the

175
00:06:02,648 --> 00:06:04,833
calculus of violence begins to run.

176
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:07,017
Colombia's World Cup is effectively over.

177
00:06:07,017 --> 00:06:09,930
They beat Switzerland in their final group game

178
00:06:09,930 --> 00:06:11,750
— a consolation, nothing more.

179
00:06:11,750 --> 00:06:12,843
They are eliminated.

180
00:06:12,843 --> 00:06:15,756
The golden generation, the Pelé-anointed world-beaters, are going

181
00:06:15,756 --> 00:06:16,120
home.

182
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:17,940
Andrés Escobar goes home too.

183
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:20,125
His family beg him not to.

184
00:06:20,125 --> 00:06:23,038
--- Before he left America, Escobar did something

185
00:06:23,038 --> 00:06:23,402
remarkable.

186
00:06:23,402 --> 00:06:24,858
He wrote a column.

187
00:06:24,858 --> 00:06:26,679
For Bogotá's El Tiempo newspaper.

188
00:06:26,679 --> 00:06:29,592
A letter to his country, from a man

189
00:06:29,592 --> 00:06:32,504
absorbing the full weight of public humiliation with

190
00:06:32,504 --> 00:06:33,961
something close to serenity.

191
00:06:33,961 --> 00:06:35,417
"Life doesn't end here.

192
00:06:35,417 --> 00:06:37,238
We have to go on.

193
00:06:37,238 --> 00:06:40,151
No matter how difficult, we must stand back

194
00:06:40,151 --> 00:06:40,515
up.

195
00:06:40,515 --> 00:06:43,428
We only have two options: either allow anger

196
00:06:43,428 --> 00:06:46,340
to paralyse us and the violence continues, or

197
00:06:46,340 --> 00:06:49,253
we overcome and try our best to help

198
00:06:49,253 --> 00:06:52,166
others." He ended it with this: "A big

199
00:06:52,166 --> 00:06:55,079
hug to everyone, and let me say it

200
00:06:55,079 --> 00:06:57,992
was a phenomenal opportunity and experience, a rare

201
00:06:57,992 --> 00:07:00,905
one, which I've never felt before in my

202
00:07:00,905 --> 00:07:03,817
life." He was writing about a football tournament.

203
00:07:03,817 --> 00:07:06,730
But he could have been writing about his

204
00:07:06,730 --> 00:07:07,458
whole country.

205
00:07:07,458 --> 00:07:10,371
Colombia received him as it receives all bad

206
00:07:10,371 --> 00:07:13,284
news — with anger, with despair, with the

207
00:07:13,284 --> 00:07:15,833
threat of violence just below the surface.

208
00:07:15,833 --> 00:07:18,382
Some fans were vicious on the street.

209
00:07:18,382 --> 00:07:19,474
Others defended him.

210
00:07:19,474 --> 00:07:20,566
He was warned.

211
00:07:20,566 --> 00:07:23,479
By friends, by family, by people who understood

212
00:07:23,479 --> 00:07:24,935
how the city worked.

213
00:07:24,935 --> 00:07:26,392
Do not go out.

214
00:07:26,392 --> 00:07:27,120
Not yet.

215
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:27,848
Not now.

216
00:07:27,848 --> 00:07:30,761
On July 2nd, 1994 — ten days after

217
00:07:30,761 --> 00:07:33,674
the own goal — Andrés Escobar went out

218
00:07:33,674 --> 00:07:34,766
with friends anyway.

219
00:07:34,766 --> 00:07:36,951
To a place called El Indio.

220
00:07:36,951 --> 00:07:38,771
He was a twenty-seven-year-old man.

221
00:07:38,771 --> 00:07:40,592
He was trying to live.

222
00:07:40,592 --> 00:07:43,505
Some time after midnight, in the parking lot

223
00:07:43,505 --> 00:07:46,418
outside El Indio, he encountered a group of

224
00:07:46,418 --> 00:07:49,330
men connected to Juan Santiago Gallón Henao and

225
00:07:49,330 --> 00:07:52,243
his brother Pedro David — narcos, gambling bosses,

226
00:07:52,243 --> 00:07:55,156
men who had reportedly lost substantial sums of

227
00:07:55,156 --> 00:07:57,341
money betting on the Colombia–USA match.

228
00:07:57,341 --> 00:07:58,797
There was a confrontation.

229
00:07:58,797 --> 00:08:01,710
And then Humberto Muñoz Castro — the Gallón

230
00:08:01,710 --> 00:08:04,623
brothers' driver and bodyguard — drew a .38

231
00:08:04,623 --> 00:08:05,351
calibre pistol.

232
00:08:05,351 --> 00:08:07,536
And shot Andrés Escobar six times.

233
00:08:07,536 --> 00:08:10,448
With every shot, it is reported, he screamed

234
00:08:10,448 --> 00:08:11,541
the same word.

235
00:08:11,541 --> 00:08:11,905
Gol.

236
00:08:11,905 --> 00:08:14,818
Andrés Escobar died forty-five minutes later in a

237
00:08:14,818 --> 00:08:15,910
hospital in Medellín.

238
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:17,731
He was twenty-seven years old.

239
00:08:17,731 --> 00:08:20,643
--- The news broke across Colombia like a

240
00:08:20,643 --> 00:08:21,372
second disaster.

241
00:08:21,372 --> 00:08:24,284
One hundred and twenty thousand people came to

242
00:08:24,284 --> 00:08:25,013
his funeral.

243
00:08:25,013 --> 00:08:25,741
120,000 people.

244
00:08:25,741 --> 00:08:28,654
In a city that had grown accustomed to

245
00:08:28,654 --> 00:08:30,474
funerals, this one stopped everything.

246
00:08:30,474 --> 00:08:31,202
Players wept.

247
00:08:31,202 --> 00:08:31,931
Reporters wept.

248
00:08:31,931 --> 00:08:34,843
Opponents from other nations left messages of condolence.

249
00:08:34,843 --> 00:08:37,756
The world football community reacted with a kind

250
00:08:37,756 --> 00:08:40,669
of stunned incomprehension — not because violence in

251
00:08:40,669 --> 00:08:43,582
Colombia was new, but because they had done

252
00:08:43,582 --> 00:08:44,674
this to him.

253
00:08:44,674 --> 00:08:45,403
The gentleman.

254
00:08:45,403 --> 00:08:48,315
The one who had responded to catastrophic failure

255
00:08:48,315 --> 00:08:51,228
with grace and wisdom and a plea for

256
00:08:51,228 --> 00:08:51,592
unity.

257
00:08:51,592 --> 00:08:54,141
They had killed the best of themselves.

258
00:08:54,141 --> 00:08:57,054
Humberto Muñoz Castro was arrested that same night.

259
00:08:57,054 --> 00:08:58,874
He confessed to the shooting.

260
00:08:58,874 --> 00:09:01,787
He was tried, convicted, and sentenced to forty-three

261
00:09:01,787 --> 00:09:02,880
years in prison.

262
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:03,972
He served eleven.

263
00:09:03,972 --> 00:09:06,885
Eleven years for the murder of Andrés Escobar.

264
00:09:06,885 --> 00:09:09,797
The Gallón brothers — the men widely believed

265
00:09:09,797 --> 00:09:12,710
to have orchestrated and ordered the killing —

266
00:09:12,710 --> 00:09:15,623
were investigated but never brought to trial on

267
00:09:15,623 --> 00:09:16,715
the murder itself.

268
00:09:16,715 --> 00:09:19,628
They spent time in custody on obstruction charges,

269
00:09:19,628 --> 00:09:22,541
yet the full weight of justice never fell

270
00:09:22,541 --> 00:09:25,454
on them for what happened in that parking

271
00:09:25,454 --> 00:09:25,818
lot.

272
00:09:25,818 --> 00:09:28,731
The system that had allowed this to happen

273
00:09:28,731 --> 00:09:30,916
protected the men who caused it.

274
00:09:30,916 --> 00:09:33,828
Escobar's girlfriend at the time, Pamela Cascardo, would

275
00:09:33,828 --> 00:09:36,741
later suggest that Muñoz Castro's eleven-year release —

276
00:09:36,741 --> 00:09:39,654
from a forty-three-year sentence — was itself evidence

277
00:09:39,654 --> 00:09:42,567
of the Gallón brothers' ability to corrupt the

278
00:09:42,567 --> 00:09:42,931
system.

279
00:09:42,931 --> 00:09:45,844
You don't serve eleven years for forty-three without

280
00:09:45,844 --> 00:09:47,664
powerful people making certain arrangements.

281
00:09:47,664 --> 00:09:50,577
No one was ever prosecuted for those arrangements.

282
00:09:50,577 --> 00:09:53,490
In the years that followed, Colombian football went

283
00:09:53,490 --> 00:09:54,946
through its long reckoning.

284
00:09:54,946 --> 00:09:57,859
The cartel money that had fuelled its golden

285
00:09:57,859 --> 00:10:00,772
age dried up — first with Pablo Escobar's

286
00:10:00,772 --> 00:10:03,685
death in December 1993, then in the aftermath

287
00:10:03,685 --> 00:10:06,598
of Andrés's murder, which triggered an international focus

288
00:10:06,598 --> 00:10:09,511
on the narco-sport relationship that could no longer

289
00:10:09,511 --> 00:10:10,239
be ignored.

290
00:10:10,239 --> 00:10:11,695
The golden generation disbanded.

291
00:10:11,695 --> 00:10:12,788
Valderrama eventually retired.

292
00:10:12,788 --> 00:10:13,516
Asprilla drifted.

293
00:10:13,516 --> 00:10:14,608
Maturana stepped down.

294
00:10:14,608 --> 00:10:17,521
And the story, as these stories often do,

295
00:10:17,521 --> 00:10:17,885
faded.

296
00:10:17,885 --> 00:10:20,798
A statue of Andrés Escobar was unveiled in

297
00:10:20,798 --> 00:10:23,711
Medellín in July 2002 — eight years after

298
00:10:23,711 --> 00:10:26,624
his death, at a Copa América held in

299
00:10:26,624 --> 00:10:26,988
Colombia.

300
00:10:26,988 --> 00:10:29,900
Children who were born after he died have

301
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:31,721
grown up touching that statue.

302
00:10:31,721 --> 00:10:34,634
--- And then, in February of 2026 —

303
00:10:34,634 --> 00:10:37,547
thirty-two years later — this story opened a

304
00:10:37,547 --> 00:10:39,731
second chapter that no one expected.

305
00:10:39,731 --> 00:10:42,644
Santiago Gallón Henao, one of the men long

306
00:10:42,644 --> 00:10:45,557
suspected of masterminding the murder of Andrés Escobar,

307
00:10:45,557 --> 00:10:48,470
was found dead in a shopping complex in

308
00:10:48,470 --> 00:10:50,654
Huixquilucan, in the State of Mexico.

309
00:10:50,654 --> 00:10:51,383
Shot once.

310
00:10:51,383 --> 00:10:54,295
Colombia's president, Gustavo Petro, confirmed the death on

311
00:10:54,295 --> 00:10:55,024
social media.

312
00:10:55,024 --> 00:10:57,936
He linked Gallón's name directly to Escobar's murder.

313
00:10:57,936 --> 00:11:00,121
He did not disguise his meaning.

314
00:11:00,121 --> 00:11:03,034
The man who was suspected of ordering the

315
00:11:03,034 --> 00:11:05,947
killing of a footballer in a parking lot

316
00:11:05,947 --> 00:11:08,860
in Medellín in 1994 was himself shot dead

317
00:11:08,860 --> 00:11:11,772
in a parking lot in Mexico in 2026.

318
00:11:11,772 --> 00:11:14,685
Whether that is justice — or just the

319
00:11:14,685 --> 00:11:17,598
mathematics of the world Gallón inhabited — is

320
00:11:17,598 --> 00:11:20,511
not a question the law can answer anymore.

321
00:11:20,511 --> 00:11:22,696
The Colombian state never convicted him.

322
00:11:22,696 --> 00:11:25,608
The cartel world eventually reached its own verdict.

323
00:11:25,608 --> 00:11:28,521
As Colombia enters the 2026 World Cup —

324
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:31,434
hosted, again, in the United States — a

325
00:11:31,434 --> 00:11:34,347
new generation of players carries the same yellow

326
00:11:34,347 --> 00:11:34,711
shirt.

327
00:11:34,711 --> 00:11:35,803
The same hope.

328
00:11:35,803 --> 00:11:38,716
The same weight of a country that still

329
00:11:38,716 --> 00:11:40,537
needs football to mean something.

330
00:11:40,537 --> 00:11:43,450
They will hear the name Andrés Escobar in

331
00:11:43,450 --> 00:11:44,178
the build-up.

332
00:11:44,178 --> 00:11:45,270
They always do.

333
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:48,183
--- Here is what stays with you, at

334
00:11:48,183 --> 00:11:50,367
the end of all of this.

335
00:11:50,367 --> 00:11:51,824
Not the own goal.

336
00:11:51,824 --> 00:11:53,644
Not the trial that failed.

337
00:11:53,644 --> 00:11:56,557
Not the narcos, or the guns, or the

338
00:11:56,557 --> 00:11:58,378
thirty-two-year chase across two continents.

339
00:11:58,378 --> 00:12:01,291
What stays with you is the column he

340
00:12:01,291 --> 00:12:01,655
wrote.

341
00:12:01,655 --> 00:12:03,111
"Life doesn't end here.

342
00:12:03,111 --> 00:12:06,024
We have to go on." He wrote that

343
00:12:06,024 --> 00:12:07,480
knowing what Medellín was.

344
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:10,393
Knowing what the world he lived in could

345
00:12:10,393 --> 00:12:10,757
do.

346
00:12:10,757 --> 00:12:13,670
Knowing, perhaps, that the men who lost money

347
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:16,583
on that game were not the type to

348
00:12:16,583 --> 00:12:17,675
accept losses quietly.

349
00:12:17,675 --> 00:12:19,132
He wrote it anyway.

350
00:12:19,132 --> 00:12:20,952
And he went home anyway.

351
00:12:20,952 --> 00:12:23,501
And he went out that night anyway.

352
00:12:23,501 --> 00:12:26,414
Because what was the alternative — to hide?

353
00:12:26,414 --> 00:12:29,327
To live smaller than the life he'd built?

354
00:12:29,327 --> 00:12:32,239
To let the violence win before it had

355
00:12:32,239 --> 00:12:33,696
even drawn its pistol?

356
00:12:33,696 --> 00:12:36,609
The goal that got him killed was not

357
00:12:36,609 --> 00:12:39,157
the own goal against the United States.

358
00:12:39,157 --> 00:12:42,070
It was every goal he ever scored for

359
00:12:42,070 --> 00:12:44,983
a country that deserved better than what it

360
00:12:44,983 --> 00:12:45,347
got.

361
00:12:45,347 --> 00:12:48,260
Every training session in Medellín when it would

362
00:12:48,260 --> 00:12:50,081
have been easier to leave.

363
00:12:50,081 --> 00:12:52,993
Every time he stayed calm in a world

364
00:12:52,993 --> 00:12:54,450
that was anything but.

365
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:57,363
Every word of that newspaper column, written in

366
00:12:57,363 --> 00:13:00,276
full knowledge that grace and reason and decency

367
00:13:00,276 --> 00:13:02,460
are never guaranteed to be enough.

368
00:13:02,460 --> 00:13:03,188
They weren't.

369
00:13:03,188 --> 00:13:05,009
But he wrote them anyway.
